body: "## Why this exists\n\nThe descriptor work was first developed while reviewing the self-host snapshot reader in #240. It changes the published engine and needs its own review. The script fixes remain with #239.\n\nSeveral reads classify a pathname and later open that name, or read an opened descriptor before checking its type. If the leaf changes between those operations, a native read can consume different bytes from the file it classified or block on a special file.\n\n## Instances and risks\n\n| Area | Existing read and consequence |\n| --- | --- |\n| Working tree and staging | The buffered and streaming paths can classify one leaf and consume another. The captured bytes, executable bit, and stat-cache entry can describe different observations. |\n| Object and pack storage | Loose, packed, and recovery reads need descriptor validation before using data. A substituted leaf can block a reader or make it consume a different object or recovery record. |\n| Refs and garbage collection | Ref values and log roots select which objects remain live. Collection must refuse substituted storage before using its bytes to authorize retirement. |\n| Erasure and destruction | Lease audits, owner records, progress records, and intent files govern authorization and recovery. A changed or nonregular descriptor must be refused before reading or acting on it. |\n| Control metadata and publication | JSON, owner markers, and recovery records are used to identify managed state. The same opened descriptor must supply the validated bytes. A captured JSON null must keep its meaning if the pathname disappears afterward. |\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Validate regular-file descriptors before reading; tie bytes, mode, and identity to the selected observation where callers rely on them.\n- Preserve supported persisted records, intentional link handling, caller-owned descriptor lifetimes, and existing recovery and write-lock semantics.\n- Exercise replacements, nonregular descriptors, premature EOF, and valid existing reads through the real engine. Include non-Git tracker coverage.\n- Keep the engine PR in draft until #240 merges. Both changes regenerate the committed self-host bundle."
